-- title:
--   Sections over a henselian base parametrised by the value of T
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a noetherian henselian local ring and let $S$ be a local ring, and let $\varphi : A[X] \to S$ be a ring homomorphism which, as an $A[X]$-algebra structure on $S$, is formally smooth, formally unramified and essentially of finite type. Let $\chi_0 : S \to \kappa$ be a ring homomorphism to the residue field $\kappa$ of $A$ whose kernel is the maximal ideal of $S$, which sends $\varphi(C\,a)$ to the residue class of $a$ for every $a \in A$, and which kills $\varphi(X)$. Two assertions are made. First, for every $c \in A$ with zero residue class, i.e. $c$ in the maximal ideal of $A$, there is exactly one ring homomorphism $\chi : S \to A$ satisfying $\chi(\varphi(C\,a)) = a$ for all $a \in A$, $\chi$ reducing to $\chi_0$ in the sense that the residue class of $\chi(f)$ equals $\chi_0(f)$ for every $f \in S$, and $\chi(\varphi(X)) = c$. Second, every ring homomorphism $\chi : S \to A$ which fixes the constants in this way and reduces to $\chi_0$ has kernel the principal ideal generated by $\varphi(X) - \varphi\bigl(C\,(\chi(\varphi(X)))\bigr)$.
--
--   This is the structure theorem for a local formally étale, essentially finite type $A[X]$-algebra over a henselian noetherian local base at a rational point: its $A$-sections lifting a given residue-field point are in bijection with the admissible values of the parameter $\varphi(X)$ in the maximal ideal, and each section cuts $S$ out by the single equation $\varphi(X) - \varphi(C\,\chi(\varphi(X)))$. It is used in the treatment of smooth-point stalks and regular prolongations on curves, and in the criterion that such an $S$ is a regular local ring.
-- source:
